## Real-World Applications

* A company could create an AI tool for writing emails.
* A business could give a model access to its own documents and information.
* A development team could make an AI assistant that understands its code and documentation.
* A custom model could be used for things like flashcards, schedules, or games.
* System prompts can also control things like tone, length, formatting, and what the model should avoid.
* A model can be run locally when privacy or company data is important.

## Personal Reflection

This lecture helped me understand what people mean when they say they made a custom AI model. I used to think that meant they trained a completely new model. The class showed that it can be much simpler than that. You can take a model that already exists and give it instructions for one job.

The system prompt was probably the most important part to me. It controls how the model is supposed to respond each time. I also thought it was interesting that changing small settings, like temperature, can change the answers.

The model did not always follow the instructions perfectly. For example, it still tried to use emojis even when it was told not to. That showed me that a custom model is still limited by the original model. It can be improved, but it will not always do exactly what you want.

## Custom Model Process Flow

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  Start["Choose Base Model"] --> File["Create Model File"]
  File --> Params["Set Parameters"]
  Params --> System["Add System Prompt"]
  System --> Copy["Copy File to Container"]
  Copy --> Create["Create Custom Model"]
  Create --> Test["Test the Model"]
  Test --> Improve{"Works Well?"}
  Improve -->|Yes| Finish["Use the Model"]
  Improve -->|No| Edit["Change Prompt or Settings"]
  Edit --> Test
```